{"parentUuid":"e9ca5e47-6d3a-4e0f-afe6-1e2306e0af69","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a66c9945e275176f8","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkZgfJggbrRVWoTR1JPq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45696,"cache_read_input_tokens":3819,"output_tokens":6101,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3909},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":45696},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6101,"cache_read_input_tokens":3819,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45696,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":45696,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":20190,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"466adb1e-33e8-47ab-ab3d-8ef69444df65","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgkZgf81v1kboaQxYvFm1","requestRef":"1ee45da8-b405-47a3-8882-cad7cf0c7246","requestedModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"5m","attributionAgent":"web-fetch","type":"assistant","uuid":"efa49222-1583-4c68-9934-5a831cd358a3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T22:24:23.722Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Semestr_9\\SIWY\\InaudibleXAI","sessionId":"7ef1ae2e-8d94-48d6-b18a-3fbdff2072e8","version":"2.1.318","gitBranch":"t1-prototype"}
{"parentUuid":"efa49222-1583-4c68-9934-5a831cd358a3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a66c9945e275176f8","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkZgfJggbrRVWoTR1JPq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I read the full text from the arXiv HTML version (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.14466, arXiv:2606.14466v1). The PDF fetch at https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.14466 returned only PDF internals, so I did not use it. The PDF was saved locally, and the harness has the path. Nothing in this report relies on the abstract alone. Several key parameters are missing from the paper's text: the margin, ε, the loss weights, and the definition of Q_i. The paper says the code, hyperparameters, and evaluation scripts are released at https://github.com/cncPomper/Audio-XAI. I did not fetch the repository.\n\n**1. \"Unchanged prediction\"**\n- The prediction constraint is top-1 label equality, not a probability or logit tolerance. The score uses an indicator 1[ŷ^orig = ŷ^adv]: \"The indicator function 1[·] acts as a strict penalty, zeroing the score if the predicted class ŷ changes.\"\n- In the optimization, the prediction is protected by \"a margin-based hinge loss (L_pred_preserve) to penalize changes in the original prediction.\" The margin value is not stated.\n- No probability or logit threshold is given, and no justification for one is offered.\n\n**2. Imperceptibility**\n- No SNR or dB bound is reported, and there is no listening test.\n- The constraint is a masking-threshold penalty: \"L_audibility(δ) = E[max(0, 20log10|F(δ)| − T(x))²], where T(x) is the static masking threshold pre-computed from the clean input.\" F is not defined in the text, and the masking model is not named.\n- A hard amplitude bound is also used: \"a hard waveform amplitude constraint δ ∈ [−ε, ε].\" The value of ε is not given.\n- Objective quality metrics are the only check: PEAQ, ViSQOL, Zimtohrli, CDPAM, PESQ, and STOI. The paper says \"High values across these indicators corroborate the minimal energy of the injected noise.\" No pass/fail thresholds are set.\n- Table 1 medians (AST / SpecTTTra / VGGish): Psychoacoustic PESQ 4.06 / 3.77 / 4.43, ViSQOL 4.64 / 4.15 / 4.89, CDPAM 0.989 / 0.981 / 0.995. PGD (the unconstrained baseline) has PESQ ≈2.76–2.84. X-Shift has PESQ 3.74–3.87.\n\n**3. Attribution-map change**\n- Two metrics are used: cosine similarity C_i = cos(A^orig, A^adv) and Top-10 overlap T_i. The paper does not define \"Top-10\" (top 10 of what, and how ties are handled).\n- They are combined into the Audio Fragility Score: AFS^stable_i = (1 − (C_i + T_i)/2) · 1[ŷ^orig = ŷ^adv] · Q_i. Q_i is described only as \"the normalized perceptual quality score\" in [0,1], with no normalization given.\n- The paper says \"an AFS^stable approaching 1.0 signifies a highly successful, imperceptible manipulation\" and 0.0 means \"a failure to shift the explanation, an altered model prediction, or unacceptable acoustic degradation.\"\n- There is no threshold for calling an attribution \"manipulated.\" AFS values themselves are not reported in the text. Only rank aggregates are given.\n\n**4. Models, data, methods, clips**\n- Models: VGGish (Hershey et al., 2017), AST (Gong et al., 2021), and SpecTTTra \"spectttra-gamma-5s\" (Rahman et al., 2025).\n- Dataset: SONICS (Rahman et al., ICLR 2025). The abstract calls the task \"audio deepfake detection,\" but SONICS is a synthetic-music dataset. Clip length, class counts, and splits are not stated in the text.\n- XAI methods: Grad-CAM and LRP.\n- Clips: \"We randomly sampled 100 recordings from the dataset, independent of the label.\" This is the only sample size reported.\n- Attacks: L∞ PGD (Madry et al., 2018), X-Shift adapted from Babadi & Karimipour (2026), and the authors' \"Psychoacoustic Noise Modeling (Ours).\" The optimizer is Adam, because the attacks need second-order derivatives.\n- The explanation loss \"minimizes the cosine similarity between the original and perturbed attribution maps.\"\n\n**5. Controls and statistics**\n- Controls are the PGD and X-Shift baselines only. No random-noise baseline is reported.\n- No confidence intervals or significance tests are reported. Table 1 gives medians with no spread. Table 2 gives \"Mean Rank (±SD)\" over model-attack combinations.\n- The easy/hard sample analysis (top 10 each, Figure 3) has no statistical test.\n- Table 2 ranks six configurations (three models, three attacks), while Table 1 has nine model-attack pairs. The ranking procedure is not explained well enough to reproduce.\n- Table 2 psychoacoustic mean rank is 4.17±1.28 with median 3.0. The text describes AST as \"the most fragile architecture (3.00±0.58),\" which ties in median with the psychoacoustic attack.\n\n**6. Headline results and limitations**\n- Headline claims: \"explanations for deepfake detection can be substantially manipulated while preserving predictions and maintaining high objective perceptual quality.\" The text states \"ViSQOL >4.1, CDPAM ≥0.98\" for the psychoacoustic attack, which matches Table 1.\n- Rankings: \"SpecTTTra demonstrated the highest resistance to explanation manipulation (Mean Rank: 7.83±0.48).\" \"AST was the most fragile architecture (3.00±0.58).\"\n- Qualitative claims without numbers: PCA shows \"smooth, directional attribution shifts for transformer-based models\" (Figure 4). The paper says \"dense, broadband signals provide a masking budget for noise.\"\n- The paper has no dedicated limitations section. The only caveats are the Impact Statement, which frames the work as a \"diagnostic tool,\" and the conclusion that \"Relying on visual attributions for audio model trustworthiness is premature.\"\n- Gaps a reviewer would raise: a single 100-clip sample, no variance across seeds, no listening test, no reported prediction-preservation rate, and reported AFS values absent from the text."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,